1. What is an Ansible Role?

An Ansible Role is a way to organize Ansible automation into separate folders and files.

When we start learning Ansible, we usually put everything inside one playbook:

Install Apache
Copy HTML file
Start Apache
Create users
Install packages

This is fine for small labs, but when the project becomes bigger, the playbook can become difficult to manage.

2. Why do we use Roles?

Imagine you have one large playbook:

tasks:
  - Install Apache
  - Copy website
  - Configure Apache
  - Restart Apache
  - Create users
  - Install monitoring tools
  - Configure firewall

As the project grows, this becomes difficult to understand and maintain.

Instead, we can separate the work:

roles/
├── apache/
├── users/
├── monitoring/
└── firewall/

Now each Role has a specific purpose.

For example:

apache Role
    ↓
Install Apache
Configure Apache
Deploy website
Restart Apache

This makes the project easier to manage.

3. Role vs Playbook

a) A Playbook is used to define what we want Ansible to do.

A Role is used to organize that work into a proper structure.

b) For example, a playbook can call an Apache role:

- hosts: webservers
  roles:
    - apache

The actual Apache work can be kept inside the apache role.

c) So, we can think of it like this:

Playbook → decides what role should run

Role → contains the actual tasks, files, templates and configuration.

d) Simple Example

=> Without Role:

site.yml
  ├── Install Apache
  ├── Copy HTML file
  ├── Configure Apache
  └── Restart Apache

=> With Role:

site.yml
     ↓
apache role
     ├── Install Apache
     ├── Copy HTML file
     ├── Configure Apache
     └── Restart Apache

4. Basic Role Directory Structure

When we create a role, Ansible provides a standard folder structure.

Each folder has a different purpose.

a) tasks/

This is where we normally write the main tasks of the role.

For example:

tasks/main.yml

It can contain tasks such as:

- name: Install Apache
  apt:
    name: apache2
    state: present

- name: Start Apache
  service:
    name: apache2
    state: started

So, tasks/ contains the actual work that Ansible needs to perform.

b) handlers/

Handlers are used for tasks that should run only when they are notified.

A common example is restarting Apache after its configuration changes.

- name: Restart Apache
  service:
    name: apache2
    state: restarted

A task can notify this handler when required.

notify: Restart Apache

This is useful because we don't need to restart a service every time the playbook runs.

c) files/

This folder is used for files that we want to copy to the managed server.

For example:

files/
└── index.html

Ansible can copy this file to the web server.

d) templates/

This folder is used for template files.

Templates normally use the Jinja2 format and can contain variables.

For example:

templates/
└── apache.conf.j2

We can use variables inside the template:

ServerName {{ server_name }}

Ansible replaces the variable with the actual value when the template is copied to the server.

e) vars/

This folder is used to store variables for the role.

Example:

apache_port: 80
apache_package: apache2

These variables can be used by the role.

f) defaults/

This is also used for variables, but these are the default values for the role.

For example:

apache_port: 80

The values in defaults/main.yml have lower priority and can normally be overridden more easily.

For beginners, remember:

defaults → default values

vars → role variables with higher priority.

g) meta/

This folder contains information about the role.

It can also be used to define dependencies.

For example, if one role requires another role to run first, that dependency can be defined here.

5. Creating a Role

We can create a role using:

ansible-galaxy init apache

This creates the standard role directory structure automatically.

6. Calling a Role from a Playbook

Once the role is created, we can call it from a playbook.

Example:

- name: Configure Web Server
  hosts: webservers
  become: true

  roles:
    - apache

Here:

a) hosts tells Ansible where to run the role.
b) become: true gives the required privilege.
c) roles tells Ansible which role to run.
d) apache is the name of our role.

Ansible will look for the apache role and execute its tasks.

7. Why Roles are Useful

Roles are mainly useful when the Ansible project becomes bigger.

They help us:

a) Keep files organized
b) Reuse automation
c) Make playbooks smaller
d) Separate different types of work
e) Make troubleshooting easier
f) Maintain large Ansible projects
g) Share automation with other people
